    {
      "stage": "Actual schema cookie and WAL trigger records",
      "cases": 82,
      "failures": 6,
      "reason": "The tbl_name field was incorrectly canonicalized to the looked-up table spelling, and IF NOT EXISTS ran before target lookup. Pinned SQLite retains the source spelling and validates the target first."
    },
    {
      "stage": "Correct spelling/priority plus fresh, signed-cookie and existing trigger reads",
      "cases": 106,
      "failures": 2,
      "reason": "The parser did not support RAISE syntax, regressing reads of existing valid C-created triggers. The parser and independent matcher now retain real action/message syntax; private marker calls are rejected and outside-trigger RAISE reports its actual context error."
    },
    {
      "stage": "Transaction visibility and overflow extensions",
      "cases": 128,
      "failures": 9,
      "reason": "Schema reads used committed disk program rows during pending DDL, and rollback catalog checking accepted only a shallow page-one catalog."
    },
    {
      "stage": "Transactional program records plus independently validated general pure catalog reader",
      "cases": 128,
      "failures": 0
    },
    {
      "stage": "Compiler/proof development",
      "reason": "Captured failures include the source-conservation let simplification, explicit trigger-body recursion/size proofs, a DDL multiline record layout and the shallow-reader compatibility simplification. Successful component/full receipts are preserved. Existing source-conservation, validating-open and page-source decode theorem statements remain unchanged."
    }
  ],
  "implementation": [
    "The source-preserving splitter uses the pinned complete.c eight-state completion grammar so only SEMI END SEMI closes a trigger. Quotes/comments remain lexical groups; the original universal source preservation laws still hold.",
    "The independent concrete grammar replays trigger names, target, timing, actual UPDATE OF names, WHEN, all body statements and terminators. The parser previously discarded UPDATE OF names; TriggerEvent now retains them. RAISE actions/messages have syntax-only private markers matched exclusively against actual RAISE tokens.",
    "Persistent trigger source is certified separately from Core row tables. Catalog state retains exact SQL/owner spelling, actual positive schema rowids, cookie and transaction/savepoint snapshots. Trigger names keep their separate namespace. Owner removal deletes its triggers; ALTER rename/drop-column and trigger firing remain explicitly unsupported.",
    "Both file encoders emit actual root-zero trigger records, and physical object checks independently compare name, owner, root, source and identity count. Actual table data/index/header checks remain mandatory before publication. schema_version reads the actual signed 32-bit connection cookie.",
    "Pending VIEW/trigger rows come from the real connection catalog and identity list. Retained table/index root records remain the actual supplied physical records; removed objects disappear. The durable-record identity theorem preserves supplied records unchanged when metadata has no pending change. New/altered table allocations remain a separate limitation.",
    "The pure page-source decoder now has a bounded general catalog traversal, including overflow payload validation, cycle/duplicate/out-of-range page checks. Its successful shallow-reader path is proved identical. Existing PageSourceDecodes/PageSourceRepresents and validating-open conclusions remain unchanged; Checked catalog evidence now names the actual general reader used by byte decoding."
  ],
  "reference_protocol": "Complete SQL output/errors and persisted catalog/rows/integrity compare against pinned SQLite in both public file modes. Fresh seeded source strings, exact trivia/name spelling, signed cookie boundaries, existing RAISE programs, open transaction/savepoint visibility and a long overflowing SQL record are exercised. No skip, expected-failure escape, oracle route or output correction is used.",
  "crash_scope": "The new 48 SIGKILL timing samples update base rows before creating a trigger, or after removing it, within one transaction. They exercise actual catalog/row/index/cookie atomicity, not trigger firing. Lean recovery precedes independent SQLite checks; both complete old and new states are observed. These samples do not establish every syscall boundary, power loss or arbitrary schedules.",
  "primary_source_basis": "Pinned src/complete.c completion transition table; src/trigger.c sqlite3BeginTrigger target/name/duplicate/system/timing order and retained target spelling; src/expr.c RAISE context diagnostic. All semantic expectations are checked by the ordinary reference executable.",
  "proof_scope": "Certified trigger parse/owner attachment and definition identity; source completion/preservation; owner cleanup preserves the database; durable physical records remain unchanged; shallow catalog compatibility; retained exact page-source/open/byte decode and publication relations. No complete trigger execution or whole-SQLite equivalence theorem is claimed.",
  "limitations": [
    "Trigger firing, BEFORE/AFTER/INSTEAD OF execution, temporary triggers, trigger-aware ALTER rename/drop-column and wider recursion/conflict/constraint/counter interactions remain work. The original trigger feature still fails in both modes.",
    "Pending sqlite_schema reads requiring newly allocated or altered table/index root pages remain unsupported; real roots are not invented. Existing schema rowid and page-geometry limitations remain.",
    "UTF-16 writes/setters and previously documented SQL/schema/storage gaps remain; finite tests do not establish global 100% compatibility. The other four named families, WITHOUT ROWID, composite primary keys and partial/expression indexes, still fail."
  ],
